Purpose: Define the full X3 scripting language so it can express smart contracts that perform all "normal contract stuff" (storage, calls, events, access control, token logic, upgradable patterns, HTLCs, cross-VM bundles). This draft targets a dual-backend approach: X3 → EVM bytecode (primary) and X3 → SVM/WASM (secondary). The spec favors determinism, auditability, and transpiler-friendly design.

1. Design goals
Deterministic semantics: Same X3 source produces identical behavior on all target VMs when semantics-preserving transpilation is used.

Compact core + rich stdlib: Small, minimal core language with powerful standard libraries (stdlib) implemented in X3 and native shims when necessary.

Gas-awareness: Language constructs are explicit about gas; compiler emits gas metering hooks for EVM.

Cross-VM friendliness: Language constructs that make cross-VM atomic bundles possible with clear boundaries.

Security-first: Patterns to avoid reentrancy, unsafe dynamic calls, predictable numeric semantics.

Readable & auditable: Human-friendly syntax with optional ABI generation and source maps.

2. High-level architecture
Front-end parser → AST

Semantic analyzer / type checker → Typed AST

Intermediate Representation (IR) → X3-IR (SSA-based, with gas annotations)

Backends:

EVM backend: IR → EVM bytecode + metadata (ABI, source map)

SVM/WASM backend: IR → WASM + bindings

