msg.data: Bytes

msg.sig: FixedBytes<4> (backend-provided)

block:

block.number: u64

block.timestamp: Timestamp

block.chainid: u64 (backend-provided)

block.basefee optional (backend-specific)

These are read-only in X3 core; no backend-specific behavior reflected in core semantics.

14. Atomic bundles & cross-VM calls (first-class)
Goal: express multi-VM atomic intent in the language, producing AtomicBundle IR nodes.

Syntax example:

atomic {
  evm.call(to: 0xabc..., data: token.transferCalldata(to, amt), gas_native: 50000);
  svm.call(module: "matcher", method: "match", args: [x, y], weight: 10);
}
Semantics:

Compiler emits an AtomicBundle with ordered subcalls.

Scheduler (node) receives bundle and executes deterministically across VMs with rollback semantics on failure.

The bundle contains per-call gas_native/weight which the scheduler maps to XU (execution units) using on-chain VMGasConfig.

Design notes:

Bundles are atomic at the chain level (all-or-nothing). Backends produce receipts for each subcall.

Bundles are signed by origin; replay protection via nonces.

